rves are stored in a shared file. The only difference might be the polling rate of the controller (how often the hardware sends data), but the game samples input once per fixed tick and ignores faster updates. So even if your PC controller reports at 1000 Hz and the Switch at 125 Hz, the game only sees the input at 60 Hz — same timing, same feel. If you use a mouse on PC and a controller on Switch, the game applies aim assist (on controller) and raw input (on mouse). But because you’re playing with different devices, you naturally expect different feels. Yet many cross‑play games adjust aim assist strength so that a skilled mouse user and a skilled controller user feel equally precise in their own ways. ### 4. **The “Tiny Differences” Are Below Your Notice** You might still ask: “But what about the one‑pixel position error after an hour?” That’s a real mathematical difference — but it’s so small that it has zero effect on gameplay. For example: - In a shooter, a 0.001‑meter position error means your crosshair is off by less than a pixel on a 1080p screen. You can’t see it. - In a platformer, your character might land 0.001 seconds later on one platform — again, undetectable. Your brain doesn’t process such tiny variances. It processes *relative* outcomes: “Did I land the jump? Did I hit the shot?” If the answer is the same across platforms, the experience feels identical. ### 5. **You Are Comparing “Feel,” Not Raw Data** When you play, you’re not measuring milliseconds or pixels. You’re comparing: - The timing of your actions (button press → on‑screen response). - The consistency of movement (does running feel the same speed?). - The feedback (sound, vibration, visual cues). Developers tune all of these to match across platforms. They literally play both versions side‑by‑side and adjust until a blindfolded tester can’t tell which is which. That’s why you’ve never noticed a difference — because the game has been deliberately steered to that outcome. ### A Quick Reali